<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiero I of Syracuse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 467 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hieron I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiero I was tyrant of Gela and then, from 478 BCE, of Syracuse, succeeding his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon</a>. In 474 BCE his fleet, allied with Cumae, destroyed an Etruscan fleet off Cumae, breaking Etruscan sea power. He moved the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> and Naxos to found the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Aetna</a> and hosted poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a> at his court.</p></div>
